var e=[{word:`Identify`,meaning:`Name, list or otherwise characterise. No development is required.`,typicalMarks:`1–2`},{word:`State`,meaning:`Express something clearly and briefly in definite terms.`,typicalMarks:`1–2`},{word:`Outline`,meaning:`Set out the main characteristics or points without full detail.`,typicalMarks:`2–4`},{word:`Describe`,meaning:`Give an account of the main features. More detail than outline, but little or no reasoning.`,typicalMarks:`2–4`},{word:`Explain`,meaning:`Set out purposes, reasons or mechanisms. Say how or why, often with a linked example.`,typicalMarks:`2–6`},{word:`Discuss`,meaning:`Present key points about different ideas or arguments and draw them together.`,typicalMarks:`6–15`},{word:`Analyse`,meaning:`Break information into parts, examine relationships, and say what the consequences or implications are.`,typicalMarks:`6–12`},{word:`Evaluate`,meaning:`Make a reasoned judgement from available evidence, weighing strengths, limitations and alternatives.`,typicalMarks:`9–15`},{word:`Justify`,meaning:`Present a reasoned case for a choice, supported by evidence, theory or statutory requirements.`,typicalMarks:`6–15`},{word:`Compare`,meaning:`Identify similarities and/or differences between two or more things.`,typicalMarks:`4–8`}],t={core:{duration:`2 hours 30 minutes`,elements:`2–6 (1 and 7 may also appear)`,notes:`One core examination, 2 hours 30 minutes, 140 marks (128 plus 12 QWC). Section A: Element 2 Supporting education and Element 6 Working in partnership (36 marks). Section B: Element 3 Safeguarding, equality and diversity and wellbeing (31). Section C: Element 4 SEND (28). Section D: Element 5 Child development (33). Elements 1 and 7 are primarily ESP but can appear in the exam. Apply named legislation and theory to UK settings.`},paperA:{duration:`2 hours 30 minutes`,elements:`2–6`,notes:`One core examination, 2 hours 30 minutes, 140 marks (128 plus 12 QWC). Section A: Element 2 Supporting education and Element 6 Working in partnership (36 marks). Section B: Element 3 Safeguarding, equality and diversity and wellbeing (31). Section C: Element 4 SEND (28). Section D: Element 5 Child development (33). Elements 1 and 7 are primarily ESP but can appear in the exam.`},paperB:{duration:`2 hours 30 minutes`,elements:`2–6`,notes:`The Generation 2 specification has a single core examination rather than Paper A and Paper B. This second practice paper still uses the same command-word mix across Elements 2–6, with Element 4 SEND and Element 5 child development weighted as they are in Sections C and D.`},esp:{duration:`15 hours 45 minutes`,notes:`The employer-set project is 15 hours 45 minutes, 90 marks, and is 40% of the core. It assesses Element 1 Wider education sector and Element 7 Professional practice, plus core skills CS1–CS4. Tasks typically include an early support or intervention plan, an activity plan, reflection on peer feedback, a presentation, and a reflective account. This practice paper compresses the thinking into three extended tasks you can attempt untimed.`},os:{notes:`Early Years Educator synoptic assignments (21–26 hours). Assignment 1 covers placement observations (including EYE competence criteria). Assignment 2 is planning educational activities. Assignment 3 includes observation, reflection and scenario-based questions (Part 2 is typically 2 hours). All five performance outcomes are assessed.`},aos:{AO1:`Recall knowledge`,AO2:`Apply knowledge to contexts`,AO3:`Analyse and evaluate`}},n=[{id:`paper-a-practice`,title:`Core examination — practice paper 1`,series:`Practice paper (NCFE spec v2.0, 2026)`,paper:`Exam`,duration:`2 hours 30 minutes`,totalMarks:88,blurb:`Command-word mix across Elements 2, 3 and 6 (Sections A and B of the core exam): supporting education, safeguarding, equality and partnership. Apply named legislation and theory to UK settings. QWC on the 15-mark question.`,sections:[{name:`Section A Supporting education and Working in partnership`,elements:`2 and 6`,questions:[{id:`pa-q1`,number:`1`,marks:1,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`2-3`],stem:`Which of the following is the statutory framework for children from birth to 5 years in England?`,options:[`The National Curriculum for Key Stage 1`,`GCSE subject specifications`,`The International Baccalaureate Primary Years Programme`,`The Early Years Foundation Stage (EYFS)`],answerIndex:3,modelAnswer:`The EYFS is the statutory framework for children from birth to 5 in England. The National Curriculum applies from Year 1 (Key Stage 1) onwards. GCSEs are Key Stage 4 qualifications. The IB PYP is not the statutory English framework.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for selecting the EYFS (option A / index 0).`]},{id:`pa-q2`,number:`2`,marks:1,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`1-1`],stem:`A childminder is best described as a practitioner who:`,options:[`Provides Ofsted-registered care and education, usually in a home-based setting, for a small number of children`,`Leads a Key Stage 2 class in a maintained primary school`,`Coordinates Education, Health and Care plans for a local authority`,`Delivers A-level teaching in a sixth-form college`],answerIndex:0,modelAnswer:`Childminders are typically Ofsted-registered (or with a childminder agency) and offer home-based provision for a small number of children. Leading a KS2 class, coordinating EHC plans and A-level teaching are different sector roles.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for selecting the home-based Ofsted-registered description (option B / index 1).`]},{id:`pa-q3`,number:`3`,marks:2,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`1-1`],stem:`Identify two differences between a private day nursery and a maintained nursery school.`,modelAnswer:`A private day nursery is usually independently owned or part of a chain, often open for full working days from baby rooms through to pre-school, and funded by a mix of parent fees and government-funded hours. A maintained nursery school is funded and overseen via the local authority, typically led by a qualified teacher, and usually offers sessional or school-day provision focused on 2-, 3- and 4-year-olds rather than all-day baby care.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for each valid difference, up to 2 marks.`,`Governance/funding: private/for-profit or charity vs local-authority maintained.`,`Leadership: nursery manager vs headteacher/qualified teacher leadership.`,`Age range/hours: often 0–5 full day vs typically 2–4 on a school-day/sessional model.`,`Curriculum accountability: both follow EYFS, but maintained nursery schools sit in the school system and are inspected as schools.`,`Do not credit vague statements such as 'they are different sizes' without a precise contrast.`]},{id:`pa-q4`,number:`4`,marks:4,ao:`AO2`,topicIds:[`2-4`,`2-5`],stem:`A Year 1 teacher uses scaffolding during a small-group phonics activity. Explain two ways this pedagogical approach can support children's educational development.`,scenario:`During a grapheme–phoneme session, the teacher models blending aloud, then invites children to blend with her, then asks them to blend independently using sound buttons. She withdraws the prompts as children succeed.`,modelAnswer:`Scaffolding, associated with Vygotsky's zone of proximal development and Bruner's work, is temporary support that enables a child to complete a task they could not yet do alone. First, modelling and shared blending show the child the process so they can internalise the strategy, which develops early reading accuracy and confidence. Second, the gradual withdrawal of prompts (the 'fade') builds independence and metacognitive control: children learn that they can blend without an adult voice, which supports progress towards National Curriculum word-reading expectations. Support that is well matched avoids both frustration (too little help) and learned helplessness (too much help).`,markScheme:[`Award up to 2 marks per explained way (identify the strategy + linked developmental benefit), maximum 4.`,`Modelling / thinking aloud so children see the blending process.`,`Working within the ZPD so the task is achievable with support (Vygotsky).`,`Fading support to promote independence and reduce over-reliance on the adult.`,`Use of concrete prompts (sound buttons, pointing) that make an abstract skill visible.`,`Building confidence / reducing cognitive load so working memory can focus on the phoneme sequence.`,`Credit other valid pedagogical links (guided participation, more knowledgeable other).`]},{id:`pa-q5`,number:`5`,marks:6,ao:`AO3`,topicIds:[`2-8`,`2-4`],stem:`Analyse how metacognition can support children and young people to manage their own learning. Use examples from an educational setting in your answer.`,modelAnswer:`Metacognition is knowledge and regulation of one's own thinking: planning an approach, monitoring understanding, and evaluating what worked (Flavell; later developed in Education Endowment Foundation guidance). In a primary classroom this might mean a Year 5 pupil pausing during a word problem to ask 'Do I understand the question?' and choosing a bar model rather than guessing. That monitoring reduces wasted effort and improves mathematical reasoning.

Metacognition also supports independence because children learn strategies they can reuse without an adult, which is essential as they move through the National Curriculum and towards secondary homework. Effective feedback (Hattie; Dweck's growth mindset) strengthens this when it comments on the process ('You checked your working') rather than only the score, so pupils attribute success to controllable strategies.

Analysis should recognise limits: young children in the EYFS are only beginning to self-regulate, so adults must model 'thinking aloud' and co-regulate first. Metacognitive prompts that are too abstract, or used without sufficient subject knowledge, become empty routines. The strongest practice therefore teaches metacognition inside real tasks, not as a bolt-on.`,markScheme:[`Award marks for developed analysis, not a list of definitions. Indicative 2 marks per well-developed point, up to 6.`,`Defines or characterises metacognition as planning, monitoring and evaluating learning.`,`Analyses a classroom example (for example checking understanding, choosing a strategy, self-marking).`,`Links metacognition to independence / managing own learning across the curriculum.`,`Connects feedback or growth mindset to better self-management.`,`Analyses a limitation or age-related constraint (EYFS co-regulation; need for subject knowledge).`,`Uses a named source or theorist appropriately (Flavell, EEF, Hattie, Dweck, Vygotsky).`]},{id:`pa-q6`,number:`6`,marks:6,ao:`AO3`,topicIds:[`5-5`,`2-7`],stem:`Analyse how personal, educational and environmental factors may affect a child's engagement in reading. Refer to at least two types of factor in your answer.`,scenario:`Maya is 6. She has glue ear, lives in a flat with little space for books, and her school has recently introduced a systematic synthetic phonics programme with a well-stocked reading corner.`,modelAnswer:`Personal factors include health and additional needs. Maya's glue ear can cause fluctuating hearing loss, which makes phoneme discrimination harder and can reduce willingness to read aloud. That personal barrier interacts with self-esteem: repeated failure at blending can lead her to avoid books.

Educational factors can protect or worsen this. A systematic synthetic phonics programme with targeted keep-up sessions gives Maya repeated, structured practice at the exact skill she finds hard, which evidence (for example Rose, EEF) associates with improved word reading. Quality feedback and matching books to her phonics phase prevent her being given texts she cannot decode.

Environmental factors include the home literacy environment. Limited space and few books reduce incidental print exposure, so Maya has fewer opportunities to practise. The school's reading corner and lending library can compensate if practitioners also work with her family (dual-language books, rhyme bags). Analysis should show that factors interact: technology such as audiobooks may support comprehension while glue ear is treated, but over-reliance on screens without talk will not build decoding. Engagement improves when all three layers are addressed together.`,markScheme:[`Award up to 2 marks for each type of factor that is analysed (not merely named), maximum 6.`,`Personal: health/SEND/hearing, motivation, self-esteem, first language, tiredness.`,`Educational: pedagogy (phonics), grouping, teacher expectations, feedback, book matching.`,`Environmental: home books, poverty, space, noise, school library, digital access.`,`Analyses interaction between factors or a compensatory strategy.`,`Applies the analysis to Maya or an equivalent named context.`,`Credit appropriate theory (Rose Review, simple view of reading, Bronfenbrenner).`]}]},{name:`Section B Safeguarding, equality and diversity and wellbeing`,elements:`3`,questions:[{id:`pa-q7`,number:`7`,marks:1,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`3-4`],stem:`Under the Children Act 1989, which statement correctly describes a child 'in need'?`,options:[`A child about whom there is reasonable cause to suspect significant harm, triggering a section 47 enquiry`,`Any child who has an Education, Health and Care plan`,`A child who is unlikely to achieve or maintain a reasonable standard of health or development without the provision of services (section 17)`,`A child who has been permanently excluded from school`],answerIndex:2,modelAnswer:`Section 17 defines a child in need: they are unlikely to achieve or maintain a reasonable standard of health or development without services. Section 47 concerns suspected significant harm ('at risk'). An EHC plan or exclusion does not by itself define 'in need'.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for selecting the section 17 description (option B / index 1).`]},{id:`pa-q8`,number:`8`,marks:1,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`3-6`],stem:`The legal definition of a 'position of trust' in relation to children is set out in which legislation?`,options:[`The Equality Act 2010`,`The Sexual Offences Act 2003`,`The Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974`,`The Data Protection Act 2018`],answerIndex:1,modelAnswer:`The Sexual Offences Act 2003 defines a position of trust (for example teachers and some other adults working with under-18s) and makes certain sexual activity with 16- and 17-year-olds in those roles a criminal offence. Later amendments closed further loopholes. The other Acts listed do not define position of trust.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for selecting the Sexual Offences Act 2003 (option C / index 2).`]},{id:`pa-q9`,number:`9`,marks:2,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`3-5`],stem:`Identify two possible physical indicators that a child may be at risk of abuse or neglect.`,modelAnswer:`Unexplained bruises, burns or injuries in unusual places (for example back, inner arms) may indicate physical abuse. Consistently poor hygiene, untreated nappy rash, or a child who is persistently hungry and underweight may indicate neglect. (Other valid indicators include untreated medical conditions, bite marks, or clothing that is inadequate for the weather.)`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for each valid physical indicator, up to 2.`,`Unexplained or patterned injuries, bruises, burns, fractures.`,`Poor hygiene, untreated conditions, persistent hunger, inadequate clothing.`,`Frequent untreated illnesses, severe nappy rash, delay in seeking medical help.`,`Do not credit behavioural indicators here (those are not physical).`]},{id:`pa-q10`,number:`10`,marks:4,ao:`AO2`,topicIds:[`3-1`,`3-1`],stem:`Explain two ways that statutory guidance such as Keeping Children Safe in Education informs policies and procedures in a primary school.`,modelAnswer:`KCSIE is statutory guidance for schools and colleges in England. First, it requires every school to have a designated safeguarding lead (DSL) and to train all staff annually on recognising abuse, so the school's safeguarding and child protection policy names the DSL, sets out how to report concerns, and schedules that training. Second, it requires safer recruitment (DBS, barred list, references) and a staff behaviour/code of conduct policy, so recruitment procedures and the low-level concerns process are written to match KCSIE rather than left to individual judgement. Policies that simply copy slogans without naming routes for referral would not meet the guidance.`,markScheme:[`Award up to 2 marks per way (what the guidance requires + how the school policy/procedure reflects it), maximum 4.`,`DSL / reporting route / annual staff training written into the child protection policy.`,`Safer recruitment, DBS and barred-list checks in the recruitment policy.`,`Staff code of conduct / acceptable use / low-level concerns procedures.`,`Managing allegations against staff (including referral to the LADO).`,`Online safety and filtering/monitoring duties reflected in the online-safety policy.`,`Credit Working Together to Safeguard Children if accurately linked to local procedures.`]},{id:`pa-q11`,number:`11`,marks:6,ao:`AO3`,topicIds:[`3-11`,`3-10`],stem:`Analyse how a child or young person's emotional health can affect their overall wellbeing and their behaviour in an educational setting.`,modelAnswer:`Emotional health underpins wellbeing because children who feel safe, valued and able to name feelings are more able to explore, concentrate and form friendships (linked to Bowlby's secure base and Maslow's belonging/safety needs). In a classroom, a child with high anxiety may present as work-avoidant, silent, or suddenly aggressive; the behaviour is a communication of unmet emotional need rather than 'naughtiness'.

Poor emotional health after bullying, neglect or family conflict can also impair working memory and sleep, which then reduces attainment and further damages self-esteem — a cyclical effect. Practitioners who only punish the behaviour without addressing the cause (emotion coaching, key person, pastoral support, possible Early Help) may worsen wellbeing.

Analysis should note the other direction too: settings that teach self-regulation, maintain predictable routines and use a trusted adult can improve emotional health, which then improves behaviour and learning. Statutory duties in KCSIE and the EYFS include promoting welfare, so emotional health is not an optional extra.`,markScheme:[`Award marks for analysis of cause-and-effect links, up to 6.`,`Links emotional health to a secure base / safety / belonging (Bowlby, Maslow or equivalent).`,`Analyses how poor emotional health may present in behaviour (withdrawal, aggression, avoidance).`,`Analyses impact on learning, friendships or physical wellbeing (sleep, somatic symptoms).`,`Identifies a cycle (behaviour sanctions without support worsen wellbeing).`,`Analyses a protective factor in the setting (key person, routines, emotion coaching).`,`Links to a statutory or professional duty to promote welfare.`]},{id:`pa-q12`,number:`12`,marks:6,ao:`AO2`,topicIds:[`3-5`,`3-1`,`3-8`],stem:`A teaching assistant in Year 2 notices that Jack, aged 7, has several bruises on his upper arms, has become withdrawn, and flinches when an adult raises a hand to point at the board. Explain the actions the practitioner should take, referring to safeguarding procedures.`,modelAnswer:`The TA should treat this as a safeguarding concern, not an informal curiosity. They should record exactly what was seen and said (date, time, description of bruises, Jack's presentation) using the school's reporting system, without photographing injuries or asking leading questions. They must report immediately to the DSL (or deputy), not wait until the end of the week and not investigate themselves by contacting parents first, because that could place Jack at further risk or contaminate evidence.

If Jack makes a disclosure, the TA should listen, not promise confidentiality, and explain they will need to share with the person who keeps children safe. The DSL decides next steps under Working Together and local procedures, which may include Early Help or a referral to children's social care if there is reasonable cause to suspect significant harm (section 47). The TA also follows the staff code: they do not gossip in the staffroom, and they continue to offer Jack calm, predictable support in class. If the concern involved a colleague rather than home, the allegation route (headteacher/LADO) would apply instead.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark per valid, applied action/explanation, up to 6.`,`Records factual observations (date, time, description) without interpretation or photos.`,`Reports immediately to the DSL / follows the setting's child protection procedure.`,`Does not investigate independently or question the child with leading questions.`,`Does not promise confidentiality if the child discloses.`,`Does not contact parents first where this could increase risk.`,`Shares information on a need-to-know basis; no staffroom gossip.`,`DSL may refer to social care / follow section 17 or 47 pathways.`,`Continues to support the child's wellbeing in class.`]}]},{name:`Section C Behaviour, self-concept and self-regulation`,elements:`5`,questions:[{id:`pa-q13`,number:`13`,marks:1,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`5-6`],stem:`According to Carl Rogers, self-concept is formed from which three elements?`,options:[`Id, ego and superego`,`Sensorimotor, pre-operational and concrete operational stages`,`Secure, avoidant and resistant attachment`,`Self-image, self-esteem and the ideal self`],answerIndex:3,modelAnswer:`Rogers described self-concept as self-image (how I see myself), self-esteem (how much I value myself) and the ideal self (how I wish to be). Id/ego/superego is Freud; the stages are Piaget; the attachment types are Ainsworth.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for selecting self-image, self-esteem and the ideal self (option B / index 1).`]},{id:`pa-q14`,number:`14`,marks:1,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`5-8`,`5-10`],stem:`Which theorist is most closely associated with children learning behaviour by observing and imitating role models?`,options:[`Albert Bandura`,`B. F. Skinner`,`Jean Piaget`,`John Bowlby`],answerIndex:0,modelAnswer:`Bandura's social learning theory (including the Bobo doll studies) argues that children model observed behaviour, especially when the model is similar, high-status or rewarded. Skinner is operant conditioning; Piaget is cognitive stages; Bowlby is attachment.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for selecting Bandura (option C / index 2).`]},{id:`pa-q15`,number:`15`,marks:2,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`5-8`],stem:`Identify two strategies practitioners use to set clear expectations of behaviour.`,modelAnswer:`Agreeing a small number of positively phrased class or setting rules with children and displaying them with visuals. Using consistent adult language and routines (for example a visual timetable and a countdown to tidy-up) so children know what 'good' looks like in that context.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for each valid strategy, up to 2.`,`Co-constructed / displayed rules or a code of conduct.`,`Visual timetable, now-and-next, or consistent routines.`,`Explicit modelling and rehearsal of expected behaviour.`,`Clear adult language; agreed consequences that match the behaviour policy.`,`Pre-correction / reminding before a high-demand transition.`]},{id:`pa-q16`,number:`16`,marks:4,ao:`AO2`,topicIds:[`5-6`,`5-6`],stem:`Explain how low self-esteem may lead to inappropriate behaviour in a Year 6 pupil. Give two developed points.`,modelAnswer:`A Year 6 pupil who sees a large gap between self-image and ideal self (Rogers) may protect themselves by avoiding tasks they might fail, which can look like work refusal or clowning to distract peers. Low self-esteem can also lead to seeking status through dominance or disruption because belonging and significance are unmet (Maslow); putting others down briefly raises the pupil's standing in the peer group even though it breaches the behaviour policy. Over time, adult responses that only punish the behaviour without repairing self-worth can confirm the pupil's belief that they are 'the naughty one', which maintains the cycle.`,markScheme:[`Award up to 2 marks per developed explanation (mechanism + linked behaviour), maximum 4.`,`Avoidance / work refusal to protect a fragile self-image.`,`Clowning or disruption to mask difficulty and gain peer attention.`,`Aggression or putting others down to gain status or control.`,`Link to Rogers (self-concept gap) or Maslow (belonging/esteem needs).`,`Self-fulfilling prophecy if adults label the child as a problem.`]},{id:`pa-q17`,number:`17`,marks:6,ao:`AO3`,topicIds:[`5-5`,`5-9`,`5-5`],stem:`Analyse how home and family circumstances can affect a child's behaviour in an early years setting.`,modelAnswer:`Family circumstances shape the child's internal working model (Bowlby) and the behaviour they bring into nursery. A toddler experiencing parental conflict or an unexpected transition such as a new baby or housing move may show regression (toileting accidents, biting) because their emotional capacity is used up on anxiety rather than self-regulation. Analysis should not assume that every child in a lone-parent or reconstituted family will have difficulties; the quality of relationships and routines matters more than the structure itself.

Care history is significant. A child who has had multiple carers or inconsistent responses may not yet trust that adults will return, so they may cling, hoard food, or test boundaries repeatedly. Bronfenbrenner's microsystem (home) interacts with the exosystem (parental shift work, poverty): tiredness and hunger are environmental factors that look like 'challenging behaviour' at 4 p.m.

Practitioners who analyse causes can adapt: a key person, predictable goodbye ritual, and partnership with parents reduce the behaviour more effectively than generic sanctions, which an under-threes child cannot yet internalise.`,markScheme:[`Award marks for analysis of links between circumstance and behaviour, up to 6.`,`Attachment / key person / inconsistent care affecting trust and boundary-testing.`,`Transitions, conflict, new sibling, housing or financial stress causing regression or dysregulation.`,`Avoids stereotyping family structures; focuses on relationship quality and routines.`,`Bronfenbrenner or similar: home, work patterns, poverty as interacting systems.`,`Physical needs (sleep, food) presenting as behaviour.`,`Implication for practice: co-regulation and partnership rather than blame.`]},{id:`pa-q18`,number:`18`,marks:6,ao:`AO2`,topicIds:[`5-11`,`5-8`,`5-12`],stem:`Harper, aged 4, shouts, throws bricks and drops to the floor at tidy-up time. Explain how practitioners can support Harper to develop self-regulation, and how they should assess any risks while responding.`,modelAnswer:`At 4, self-regulation is still developing (linked to maturation of executive function), so adults should co-regulate first. A practitioner can give a five-minute visual warning, use a now-and-next board, and offer a defined job ('You are the brick-basket helper') so Harper has a positive role rather than an abrupt stop to play. Emotion coaching (labelling 'You are cross because play is finishing') plus a calm, low voice models the strategy Harper can later use alone.

If Harper throws bricks, staff follow the behaviour policy: move other children from the path of objects, use a planned script, and avoid physical intervention unless there is immediate danger, in line with the setting's risk assessment and Use of Reasonable Force guidance. After the incident, a restorative conversation at Harper's level and a review of whether tidy-up is too sudden or too noisy reduces repeat risk. Recording ABC (antecedent, behaviour, consequence) helps the team see that the trigger is the transition, not 'defiance', so the plan can be adjusted.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark per valid applied explanation, up to 6.`,`Co-regulation / emotion coaching / naming feelings.`,`Advance warning, visual timetable, now-and-next, or defined helper role.`,`Positive phrasing and specific praise when Harper begins to help.`,`Keep other children safe; remove objects that could cause injury.`,`Follow policy; physical intervention only if necessary to prevent harm; dynamic risk assessment.`,`ABC recording / restorative follow-up / review of the transition itself.`,`Credit Zones of Regulation, sensory breaks or a quiet space if applied to Harper.`]}]},{name:`Section D Working in partnership`,elements:`6`,questions:[{id:`pa-q19`,number:`19`,marks:1,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`6-2`],stem:`A reconstituted (blended) family is best described as a family in which:`,options:[`Two parents of the same sex raise a child together from birth`,`Grandparents have a court order to care for a child full-time`,`A parent and their child live with a new partner, who may also have children`,`A child is looked after by the local authority in a residential home`],answerIndex:2,modelAnswer:`A reconstituted or blended family forms when a parent re-partners and households combine, often including step-siblings. Same-sex parenting from birth is a nuclear/same-sex-parent family; kinship care and looked-after arrangements are different structures.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for selecting the blended/reconstituted description (option B / index 1).`]},{id:`pa-q20`,number:`20`,marks:2,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`6-3`],stem:`Identify two possible barriers to effective partnership with parents or carers.`,modelAnswer:`Parents whose own school experiences were negative may distrust practitioners and avoid meetings. Work patterns, lack of transport, or limited English without an interpreter can make it practically difficult to engage, even when parents want to be involved.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for each valid barrier, up to 2.`,`Language / literacy / no interpreter or translated information.`,`Working hours that clash with typical pick-up or parents' evening times.`,`Previous negative experiences of education or of professionals.`,`Fear of judgement, stigma (for example around SEND, poverty, or social care).`,`Digital poverty, no credit for online apps, or unstable housing.`,`Cultural mismatch or practitioners making assumptions about family life.`]},{id:`pa-q21`,number:`21`,marks:4,ao:`AO2`,topicIds:[`6-1`,`6-4`],stem:`A 3-year-old has started at nursery and is finding mornings distressing. Explain two advantages of working in partnership with the child's parents to support settling in.`,modelAnswer:`Parents hold unique knowledge of the child's comfort objects, sleep, language and previous separations. Sharing this in a settling plan (key person, shorter first sessions, parent staying to model a calm goodbye) reduces the child's stress and helps the key person provide a secure base more quickly. Partnership also builds parental confidence: when staff share brief, honest updates and signpost reliable sources such as the NHS, Family Hubs or the setting's key-person communication book, parents are more likely to continue a consistent goodbye routine at home, which benefits the child's emotional development across both environments.`,markScheme:[`Award up to 2 marks per advantage (what the partnership does + why it helps the child/family), maximum 4.`,`Parents' unique knowledge informs a personalised settling plan.`,`Consistent routines between home and setting reduce anxiety.`,`Builds trust so parents share concerns early (health, SEND, home changes).`,`Supports the child's secure attachment with the key person.`,`Enables signposting to reliable resources (Family Hub, health visitor, IASS).`]},{id:`pa-q22`,number:`22`,marks:6,ao:`AO2`,topicIds:[`6-5`,`6-5`,`6-5`],stem:`A speech and language therapist (SLT) is visiting the nursery to assess a 2-year-old. Explain how the early years practitioner should work collaboratively with the SLT while maintaining professional boundaries.`,modelAnswer:`Collaborative working means the practitioner prepares relevant, factual observations (words the child uses, how they communicate at snack, any hearing concerns) and gains informed parental consent before sharing them, in line with UK GDPR and the setting's information-sharing policy. During the visit they facilitate the session without taking over, model strategies the SLT recommends (for example commenting rather than quizzing), and agree how targets will be used in everyday play.

Professional boundaries require the practitioner not to offer a diagnosis, not to share other families' information, and not to give the SLT their personal mobile number or discuss the child on social media. They should stay within their role: they are the child's key person, not a therapist. After the visit they record agreed next steps, feed back to parents as planned with the SLT, and raise any safeguarding issues through the DSL rather than via informal chat. This protects the child, the family and the professional relationship.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark per valid applied point, up to 6.`,`Parental consent and need-to-know information sharing (GDPR / setting policy).`,`Provides accurate observations to inform the SLT assessment.`,`Implements and embeds SLT strategies in daily routines.`,`Does not diagnose or work outside competence.`,`Maintains confidentiality; no social media or personal contact details.`,`Agrees roles, records next steps, and feeds back to parents appropriately.`,`Safeguarding concerns go through the DSL, not informal side conversations.`]},{id:`pa-q23`,number:`23`,marks:15,ao:`AO3`,qwc:!0,topicIds:[`6-4`,`6-5`,`6-5`,`6-1`,`3-4`],stem:`Discuss the importance of multi-agency working in supporting children, young people and families. Analyse both the benefits and the challenges, and refer to legislation or statutory guidance in your answer.`,scenario:`You may write in relation to early years, primary or secondary provision. Quality of written communication will be assessed.`,modelAnswer:`Level 3 / high-band response:

Multi-agency working means practitioners from education, health, social care and the voluntary sector sharing information and planning together so that children and families receive coordinated support. It is grounded in the Children Act 2004 (the duty to cooperate to improve wellbeing), Working Together to Safeguard Children, and, for pupils with SEND, the Children and Families Act 2014 and the SEND Code of Practice 2015, which require a person-centred, joined-up approach from 0–25.

Benefits are both protective and developmental. For a child in need (Children Act 1989, s.17), Early Help that includes the health visitor, setting, and a Family Hub can prevent escalation to child protection. For a child with speech delay, the SLT, key person and parent using the same strategies multiplies practice opportunities. Information sharing, when lawful and proportionate (UK GDPR; KCSIE), reduces the risk that each agency holds only part of the picture — a lesson repeatedly drawn from serious case reviews where agencies failed to join the dots. Families also benefit from not having to repeat their story to every professional, which can reduce stigma and improve engagement.

Challenges must be weighed, not ignored. Professional jargon, different thresholds (for example social care vs education), and part-time visiting professionals can fragment rather than integrate support. Confidentiality is a genuine tension: practitioners must share when a child is at risk, but over-sharing damages trust. Power imbalances may sideline parental voice, especially where English is an additional language or parents fear that asking for help will trigger social-care involvement. Time and funding constraints mean TAC (team around the child) meetings can become tokenistic if actions are not followed up.

Evaluation: multi-agency working is not automatically effective. It is important when roles are clear, a lead professional coordinates, the child's lived experience is central, and information is shared with consent or under a safeguarding duty. Used well, it is one of the strongest tools for both welfare and learning; used poorly, it can delay action and overwhelm families. Practitioners therefore need professional boundaries, accurate records, and the confidence to challenge other agencies if the child's needs are not being met.`,markScheme:[`QWC: credit spelling, structure, specialist vocabulary and a coherent argument on the 15-mark response.`,`Band 1 (1–5): Brief description of agencies or benefits with little analysis; list-like; limited or inaccurate law.`,`Band 2 (6–10): Some analysis of benefits and at least one challenge; some application to children/families; one relevant statute or guidance named.`,`Band 3 (11–15): Sustained discussion of benefits and challenges; named legislation/guidance used accurately; evaluation of when multi-agency working is effective; clear QWC.`,`Indicative content — benefits: holistic picture; early help; SEND/EHC coordination; reduced family burden; safeguarding (joining the dots).`,`Indicative content — challenges: thresholds, jargon, time, funding, confidentiality vs sharing, parental fear, tokenistic meetings.`,`Legislation/guidance: Children Act 1989/2004, Working Together, KCSIE, CFA 2014, SEND CoP 2015, UK GDPR, Equality Act 2010.`,`Agencies/roles: social care, health visitor, SLT, CAMHS, EP, SENCO, DSL, Family Hub, police, GP, portage.`,`Judgement: effectiveness depends on lead professional, clear roles, child's voice, lawful sharing.`]}]}]},{id:`paper-b-practice`,title:`Core examination — practice paper 2`,series:`Practice paper (NCFE spec v2.0, 2026)`,paper:`Exam`,duration:`2 hours 30 minutes`,totalMarks:88,blurb:`Command-word mix weighted to Sections C and D of the core exam: Element 4 SEND and Element 5 child development, plus EAL from Element 2. Same command-word diet, including one 15-mark QWC question.`,sections:[{name:`Section A Child development`,elements:`5`,questions:[{id:`pb-q1`,number:`1`,marks:1,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`5-1`],stem:`According to Piaget, children in the pre-operational stage are typically aged:`,options:[`Birth to 2 years`,`7 to 11 years`,`11 years and over`,`2 to 7 years`],answerIndex:3,modelAnswer:`Piaget's pre-operational stage is approximately 2–7 years (symbolic play, egocentrism, centration). Sensorimotor is 0–2, concrete operational 7–11, formal operational 11+.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for selecting 2 to 7 years (option B / index 1).`]},{id:`pb-q2`,number:`2`,marks:1,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`5-2`],stem:`Bowlby's idea that early relationships create a template for later relationships is known as the:`,options:[`Zone of proximal development`,`Internal working model`,`Hierarchy of needs`,`Looking-glass self`],answerIndex:1,modelAnswer:`Bowlby proposed an internal working model: the infant's first attachment relationship becomes a mental template for trust, worth and later adult relationships. ZPD is Vygotsky; hierarchy of needs is Maslow; looking-glass self is Cooley.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for selecting internal working model (option C / index 2).`]},{id:`pb-q3`,number:`3`,marks:2,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`5-4`],stem:`Identify two differences between receptive language and expressive language.`,modelAnswer:`Receptive language is understanding words, sentences and instructions; expressive language is using words, signs or other means to communicate. Receptive skills typically develop ahead of expressive skills, so a toddler may follow 'get your shoes' before they can say the sentence themselves.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for each valid difference, up to 2.`,`Receptive = understanding; expressive = producing language.`,`Receptive usually develops earlier / is often stronger than expressive.`,`Difficulties may appear in one without the other (for example understands but rarely speaks).`,`Expressive can include speech, sign or AAC; receptive includes listening and reading of gesture/context.`]},{id:`pb-q4`,number:`4`,marks:4,ao:`AO2`,topicIds:[`5-4`],stem:`Explain two ways practitioners can promote language development in children under 2 years.`,modelAnswer:`Serve-and-return interaction (treating the baby's babbles as turns in a conversation) builds the foundations of dialogue and attachment, which in turn fuel language. Practitioners should get down to the child's level, leave a pause, and respond with a slightly expanded model ('Ball. You have the red ball') rather than a barrage of closed questions. Sharing books and rhymes daily, naming what the child is looking at, also maps words onto real experiences and supports both receptive vocabulary and later phonological awareness. These strategies align with EYFS communication and language and with evidence on contingent talk.`,markScheme:[`Award up to 2 marks per way (strategy + why it promotes language), maximum 4.`,`Serve-and-return / commenting / expanding utterances.`,`Reduced questions; increased running commentary during care routines.`,`Songs, rhymes, books, and following the child's gaze.`,`Key person, home language valued, or parent partnership to increase input.`,`Credit Trevarthen, Hart and Risley (cautiously), or CSL if accurately applied.`]},{id:`pb-q5`,number:`5`,marks:6,ao:`AO3`,topicIds:[`5-2`,`5-3`],stem:`Analyse how the quality of a child's early attachments may influence their friendships and relationships in later childhood.`,modelAnswer:`Ainsworth's Strange Situation identified secure, insecure-avoidant and insecure-resistant patterns; Main and Solomon later described disorganised attachment. A securely attached child has an internal working model of adults as reliable and of the self as worthy of care (Bowlby). In middle childhood that child is more likely to approach peers with trust, recover from playground conflict, and use adults as a secure base when friendships wobble.

An insecure-avoidant child may appear independent but under-use adults and keep friendships at a distance, while an insecure-resistant child may be clingy, jealous or easily slighted. Disorganised attachment, often associated with frightening or frightened care, can make peer relationships chaotic and is a risk factor for later social and emotional difficulties.

Analysis should avoid determinism: later relationships, a consistent key person or teacher, and friendships themselves can offer earned security (Rutter's work on protective factors). Practitioners therefore observe relationship patterns without labelling the child as 'insecure', and they provide predictable, sensitive care that can still reshape the working model.`,markScheme:[`Award marks for analysis, up to 6.`,`Names attachment patterns (Ainsworth / Main) or internal working model (Bowlby).`,`Analyses how secure attachment supports later friendships and help-seeking.`,`Analyses at least one insecure pattern and its likely social presentation.`,`Avoids saying early attachment rigidly determines adult life.`,`Identifies protective factors (key person, later relationships, Rutter).`,`Implication for practice: sensitive, predictable care rather than labelling.`]},{id:`pb-q6`,number:`6`,marks:6,ao:`AO3`,topicIds:[`5-9`,`5-1`],stem:`Analyse the difference between expected and unexpected transitions, and how each may affect children. Use examples.`,modelAnswer:`Expected transitions are predictable, culturally typical changes that settings can prepare for: starting nursery, moving from Reception to Year 1, puberty, or transfer to secondary school. Because they are anticipated, practitioners can use visits, social stories, visual timetables and parent partnership to keep the child's secure base intact. Effects still occur — sleep disruption, clinginess, a dip in attainment — but they are usually time-limited if the transition is well planned.

Unexpected transitions are unplanned: bereavement, parental separation, sudden housing move, a key person leaving, or a child becoming looked after. The child has had no rehearsal, so the same behaviours (regression, anger, withdrawal) may be more intense and prolonged. Analysis should note that the same event can be expected for one child (a planned house move in the summer) and unexpected for another (emergency homelessness).

Age mediates impact: a baby may protest at a change of carer without understanding why; an adolescent may understand a parental separation cognitively but still experience it as a threat to identity and friendships. High-quality practice treats every transition as a potential developmental turning point, not a purely administrative move.`,markScheme:[`Award marks for a clear contrast plus analysed effects, up to 6.`,`Defines expected transitions with an example.`,`Defines unexpected transitions with an example.`,`Analyses typical effects (regression, anxiety, behaviour, learning dip).`,`Analyses why unexpected transitions may be more destabilising.`,`Age/stage or individual difference in how transitions are experienced.`,`Practice implication: preparation, key person, partnership, extra co-regulation.`]}]},{name:`Section B Professional practice — assessment and reflection`,elements:`7`,questions:[{id:`pb-q7`,number:`7`,marks:1,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`7-1`],stem:`What is the main purpose of the Year 1 phonics screening check in England?`,options:[`To award a GCSE English grade`,`To replace the EYFS profile with a standardised IQ score`,`To confirm whether pupils can decode using phonics to an expected standard and to identify who needs extra support`,`To decide which pupils should be permanently excluded`],answerIndex:2,modelAnswer:`The phonics screening check is a national benchmark to see whether Year 1 pupils can decode to an expected standard, so that those who cannot are given additional support (and retake in Year 2). It is not a GCSE, IQ test, or an exclusion tool.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for selecting the decoding/extra-support purpose (option B / index 1).`]},{id:`pb-q8`,number:`8`,marks:1,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`7-5`],stem:`In Gibbs' reflective cycle, which stage comes immediately after 'description'?`,options:[`Feelings`,`Action plan`,`Abstract conceptualisation`,`Reflection-in-action`],answerIndex:0,modelAnswer:`Gibbs' cycle is description, feelings, evaluation, analysis, conclusion, action plan. Abstract conceptualisation is Kolb; reflection-in-action is Schön.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for selecting feelings (option B / index 1).`]},{id:`pb-q9`,number:`9`,marks:2,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`7-2`],stem:`Identify two differences between formative and summative assessment.`,modelAnswer:`Formative assessment is ongoing and used to inform next steps in teaching (for example observing a child's mixing of paints and then providing primary colours the next day). Summative assessment sums up learning at a point in time against a standard (for example the EYFS profile at the end of Reception, or Key Stage 2 tests).`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for each valid difference, up to 2.`,`Formative = ongoing / Assessment for Learning; summative = snapshot at the end of a period.`,`Formative informs next steps; summative reports attainment against a benchmark.`,`Formative is often informal (observation, questioning); summative may be standardised or reported nationally.`,`Timing: during learning vs at the end of a unit, year or key stage.`]},{id:`pb-q10`,number:`10`,marks:4,ao:`AO2`,topicIds:[`7-3`,`7-4`],stem:`Explain why it is important that practitioners accurately observe, record and report on a child's participation and progress.`,modelAnswer:`Accurate observation gives a reliable picture of what the child can do in real contexts, which supports fair next-step planning and avoids decisions based on a single bad morning. Factual records (dated, distinguishing observation from interpretation) enable the key person, SENCO and parents to see patterns over time — for example a dip after a transition — and to evidence whether support is working. Reporting accurately to parents and, where relevant, to other professionals meets professional and statutory duties (EYFS assessment requirements; SEND graduated approach) and protects the child from both missed need and unnecessary labelling.`,markScheme:[`Award 1–2 marks per developed reason, maximum 4.`,`Informs planning and next steps matched to the child.`,`Creates an evidence trail for parents, SENCO, or multi-agency support.`,`Distinguishes fact from opinion; reduces bias.`,`Meets EYFS / school assessment / SEND Code of Practice duties.`,`Identifies progress, regression, or barriers (including safeguarding concerns).`]},{id:`pb-q11`,number:`11`,marks:6,ao:`AO3`,topicIds:[`7-5`,`7-6`],stem:`Analyse how using a model of reflection can help a practitioner improve their practice after a difficult session.`,scenario:`A student practitioner led a group story time. Two children left the carpet, and the practitioner felt the session had failed.`,modelAnswer:`A structured model stops reflection collapsing into self-blame. Using Gibbs, the practitioner first describes the session factually, then acknowledges feelings of embarrassment, then evaluates what went well (some children joined in the refrain) as well as what did not. Analysis might draw on child development: a 10-minute story without props exceeded some 3-year-olds' attention, and the seating meant one child could not see. Conclusion and action plan produce concrete change: a shorter text, a visual cue for sitting, and a colleague to co-regulate the child who leaves.

Schön's reflection-in-action would also have value: noticing mid-session that children were restless and switching to a stand-up rhyme. Ongoing developmental feedback from a mentor (Element 9) makes the model more powerful than reflecting alone, because an experienced other can challenge assumptions ('Was it failure, or an over-ambitious plan?'). Analysis should note a limit: models become tick-box if the action plan is never tried. Improvement depends on cycling Kolb-style through another concrete experience.`,markScheme:[`Award marks for analysis applied to the scenario, up to 6.`,`Names a model accurately (Gibbs, Kolb, Schön, Driscoll, Atkins and Murphy, Johns).`,`Shows how stages move the practitioner from emotion to action.`,`Applies the model to story time (attention, environment, planning).`,`Links reflection to feedback / mentor / CPD.`,`Analyses a limitation (tokenistic reflection; need to act).`,`Identifies a specific improvement that could be tried next time.`]},{id:`pb-q12`,number:`12`,marks:6,ao:`AO2`,topicIds:[`7-7`,`2-2`],stem:`Explain how practitioners can plan their own professional development, and why this benefits children and young people.`,modelAnswer:`Practitioners meet developmental needs through a mix of mandatory training (safeguarding, paediatric first aid), supervision, peer observation, and self-directed CPD such as reading the EYFS reforms or attending a local authority language briefing. A personal development plan that uses SMART targets, plus honest feedback from a mentor, turns experience into skill rather than repeating the same year of practice.

This matters for children because the quality of adult–child interaction is the strongest in-setting driver of outcomes. An adult who has updated their knowledge of systematic phonics, or of trauma-informed behaviour, will make better in-the-moment decisions. It also models lifelong learning and meets professional expectations (Early Years Educator criteria; Teachers' Standards where relevant). Remaining current on debates — for example screen time, inclusion, or the place of play — helps practitioners justify practice rather than following fads uncritically.`,markScheme:[`Award marks for methods plus a linked reason why children benefit, up to 6.`,`Supervision, mentoring, or professional discussion.`,`Mandatory and additional CPD (safeguarding, first aid, curriculum).`,`Peer observation, reading research, professional networks.`,`Personal development planning / SMART targets / reflection.`,`Impact on quality of interaction, safety, or curriculum for children.`,`Keeps practice current with policy debates and statutory changes.`]}]},{name:`Section C Equality, diversity and inclusion`,elements:`3`,questions:[{id:`pb-q13`,number:`13`,marks:1,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`3-1`],stem:`Which of the following is a protected characteristic under the Equality Act 2010?`,options:[`Social class`,`Being a young carer`,`Living in rural poverty`,`Disability`],answerIndex:3,modelAnswer:`Disability is one of the nine protected characteristics. Social class, young carer status and rural poverty can create disadvantage and should be addressed through inclusion policy, but they are not listed as protected characteristics in the Act.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for selecting disability (option B / index 1).`]},{id:`pb-q14`,number:`14`,marks:1,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`3-12`],stem:`Labelling a child as 'the naughty one' is most likely to contribute to:`,options:[`An automatic diagnosis of SEMH needs`,`A self-fulfilling prophecy of continued inappropriate behaviour`,`Higher teacher expectations and faster progress`,`A legal requirement to issue an EHC plan`],answerIndex:1,modelAnswer:`Negative labels can shape adult expectations and the child's self-concept so that the behaviour is maintained (self-fulfilling prophecy; Rosenthal and Jacobson). A label is not a diagnosis or an EHC trigger, and it does not raise expectations.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for selecting self-fulfilling prophecy (option A / index 0).`]},{id:`pb-q15`,number:`15`,marks:2,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`3-13`],stem:`Identify two barriers that may prevent a child or young person from participating fully in an educational setting.`,modelAnswer:`Physical barriers such as steps with no ramp, or toilets that a child who uses a wheelchair cannot access. Attitudinal barriers such as staff assuming a child with EAL cannot join a philosophy discussion, which lowers opportunity.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for each valid barrier, up to 2.`,`Physical / sensory environment (access, acoustics, lighting).`,`Attitudinal (stereotypes, low expectations, prejudice).`,`Organisational (inflexible routines, untranslated letters, cost of trips).`,`Social or economic (poverty, no transport, digital exclusion).`,`Language / communication barriers, including lack of AAC.`]},{id:`pb-q16`,number:`16`,marks:4,ao:`AO2`,topicIds:[`3-1`,`3-11`],stem:`Explain two reasons why it is important for a school or nursery to promote equality, diversity and inclusion, referring to legal or organisational requirements.`,modelAnswer:`The Equality Act 2010 prohibits direct and indirect discrimination, harassment and victimisation in relation to protected characteristics, and the public sector equality duty requires schools to eliminate discrimination and foster good relations. Promoting inclusion in policy and daily practice (for example diverse books, accessible toilets, challenging racist language) is therefore a legal duty, not a preference. Second, inclusion has educational and social value: children who see their identity respected have a stronger sense of belonging, which supports wellbeing and attainment, while peers learn to value difference. Organisational policies on behaviour, SEND, and anti-bullying must align with the Act so that staff responses are consistent rather than dependent on individual goodwill.`,markScheme:[`Award up to 2 marks per reason, maximum 4.`,`Equality Act 2010 / public sector equality duty / UNCRC Article 2.`,`Policies must match legal duties (anti-bullying, SEND, admissions).`,`Belonging, wellbeing and attainment benefits for children.`,`Prepares all children for life in a diverse society.`,`Reduces discrimination, exclusion, and safeguarding harms linked to hate incidents.`]},{id:`pb-q17`,number:`17`,marks:6,ao:`AO3`,topicIds:[`3-12`,`4-4`],stem:`Analyse the possible consequences of labelling children and young people. Consider both risks and any arguments that identification can help.`,modelAnswer:`Labels can harm. Calling a child 'slow', 'naughty' or even using a diagnosis as a shorthand for the whole person can lower adult expectations, restrict the activities offered, and become internalised in the child's self-concept (Rogers; self-fulfilling prophecy). Peers may socially exclude a labelled child, which damages friendships and emotional health. Language matters: person-first or identity-first terms should follow the child's and family's preference, and slang or deficit language is never appropriate.

However, accurate identification is not the same as a stigmatising nickname. A recognised SEND need can unlock reasonable adjustments, an EHC needs assessment, and specialist teaching — a protective consequence if handled through the social model (removing barriers) rather than the medical model (the child as the problem). Analysis should conclude that practitioners must describe needs and strengths precisely, review labels that no longer fit, and never let a category replace knowing the individual child.`,markScheme:[`Award marks for a balanced analysis, up to 6.`,`Risks: lowered expectations, self-concept, stigma, restricted opportunities.`,`Self-fulfilling prophecy / Rosenthal or equivalent.`,`Peer exclusion or adult talk that reduces the child to the label.`,`Counter-argument: identification can unlock support, adjustments, funding.`,`Distinguishes stigmatising labels from accurate, reviewed identification.`,`Practice: strengths-based language, social model, child's identity respected.`]},{id:`pb-q18`,number:`18`,marks:6,ao:`AO2`,topicIds:[`3-1`,`3-13`],stem:`A primary school's equality policy is displayed in the office but lunchtime staff still allow boys to dominate the football pitch and tell girls to 'go and skip'. Explain how organisational policies should link to daily practice, and how this situation could be improved.`,modelAnswer:`Policies only meet the Equality Act if they are enacted. The public sector equality duty and the school's behaviour and PE policies should lead to rotas, alternative games, and staff training so that access to popular play is not gated by sex. Lunchtime staff need the same briefing as teachers: sex is a protected characteristic, and steering girls away from football is a form of discrimination even if it is 'how we have always done it'.

Improvement is practical as well as cultural. The PE lead and senior staff should observe lunchtimes, add zoning or a booking system, provide a second ball game, and praise inclusive play. Pupil voice (a playground council that includes girls who want to play football) makes the policy real. Recording and challenging comments, then following the anti-bullying/equality procedure, shows children that the policy has teeth. Without this, the written policy is a paper barrier rather than a tool for participation.`,markScheme:[`Award marks for applied explanation, up to 6.`,`Identifies the mismatch between policy and practice as a problem.`,`Links to Equality Act 2010 (sex; possible indirect discrimination).`,`Staff training / consistent expectations for lunchtime team.`,`Environmental or organisational change (rotas, zones, extra equipment).`,`Pupil voice and adult modelling of inclusive play.`,`Use of behaviour/anti-bullying procedures when comments persist.`]}]},{name:`Section D SEND and EAL`,elements:`4 and 2`,questions:[{id:`pb-q19`,number:`19`,marks:1,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`4-1`],stem:`The SEND Code of Practice 2015 graduated approach is best summarised as:`,options:[`Exclude, refer, discharge, close`,`Diagnose, medicate, withdraw, retest`,`Assess, plan, do, review`,`Observe, label, stream, report`],answerIndex:2,modelAnswer:`The SEND Code of Practice sets out a cycle of assess, plan, do, review. It is not a medical or streaming model and does not start with exclusion.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for selecting assess, plan, do, review (option B / index 1).`]},{id:`pb-q20`,number:`20`,marks:2,ao:`AO1`,topicIds:[`4-9`],stem:`Identify two differences between the medical model and the social model of disability.`,modelAnswer:`The medical model locates disability in the individual's impairment and focuses on treatment or 'fixing' the person. The social model (Oliver and others) locates disability in barriers created by society — inaccessible buildings, inflexible pedagogy, negative attitudes — and focuses on removing those barriers so the person can participate.`,markScheme:[`Award 1 mark for each valid difference, up to 2.`,`Medical: problem in the person; social: problem in the environment/society.`,`Medical: cure/treat; social: remove barriers and adjust practice.`,`Medical: expert-led; social: rights, participation, child's voice.`,`Credit an example that makes the contrast clear (ramp vs expecting the child to walk).`]},{id:`pb-q21`,number:`21`,marks:4,ao:`AO2`,topicIds:[`4-10`,`4-7`],stem:`Explain two ways that augmentative and alternative communication (AAC) can support a child whose speech does not yet meet their communication needs.`,modelAnswer:`Unaided AAC such as Makaton signs, used alongside spoken words by all adults, gives the child a motor pattern they can use when speech is effortful, which reduces frustration and behaviour that communicates unmet need. Aided AAC such as a core board, PECS, or a voice-output device lets the child initiate requests, comments and refusals, which develops expressive language and autonomy rather than waiting to be guessed. Importantly, AAC supplements speech; it does not 'stop children talking' when adults continue to model spoken language alongside it.`,markScheme:[`Award up to 2 marks per way, maximum 4.`,`Unaided AAC (sign, gesture) modelled with speech.`,`Aided AAC (symbols, PECS, VOCAs, eye-gaze) for initiating communication.`,`Reduces frustration and supports behaviour and wellbeing.`,`Promotes participation, choice and inclusion with peers.`,`Addresses the myth that AAC delays speech; it can support language development.`]},{id:`pb-q22`,number:`22`,marks:6,ao:`AO3`,topicIds:[`2-9`,`2-10`,`2-12`],stem:`Analyse why maintaining a child's home language is important while they acquire English as an additional language, and how practitioners can support both.`,modelAnswer:`Home language is a cognitive and emotional asset, not a barrier. Cummins' work suggests that conceptual knowledge and literacy skills transfer between languages (common underlying proficiency), so a child who can discuss a story in Urdu is better placed to later discuss it in English. Stopping the home language can delay CALP, damage identity and weaken family relationships, because parents may not be able to support homework or comfort the child in English.

The five stages of additional-language acquisition include a silent period. Practitioners who insist on English-only at that stage misread silence as non-compliance. Support includes dual-language books, visual timetables, bilingual staff or recorded greetings, pre-teaching vocabulary, and encouraging families to keep rich talk in the home language. Analysis should reject the myth that two languages confuse young children; simultaneous bilingualism is typical worldwide. The practitioner's job is to add English, not replace the language that carries attachment and culture.`,markScheme:[`Award marks for analysis, up to 6.`,`Home language supports identity, attachment and family communication.`,`Transfer of concepts / Cummins BICS and CALP or equivalent.`,`Silent period as a typical stage, not a deficit.`,`Risks of an English-only approach (loss, shame, delayed meaning-making).`,`Concrete strategies: visuals, dual-language resources, family partnership, pre-teaching.`,`Challenges the 'two languages confuse children' misconception.`]},{id:`pb-q23`,number:`23`,marks:15,ao:`AO3`,qwc:!0,topicIds:[`4-1`,`4-3`,`4-9`,`2-11`,`2-12`,`3-11`],stem:`Evaluate strategies a Reception class could use to support a child who has special educational needs and is also acquiring English as an additional language. Refer to legislation, models of disability or language acquisition theory in your answer.`,scenario:`Yusuf is 4 years 10 months. He has recently arrived from overseas, has a recognised hearing impairment, and currently uses a few single English words. He watches peers closely and enjoys construction. Quality of written communication will be assessed.`,modelAnswer:`Level 3 / high-band response:

Yusuf's needs sit across SEND and EAL, which must not be collapsed into one category. The Children and Families Act 2014 and SEND Code of Practice 2015 require a graduated assess–plan–do–review cycle, with the child and family at the centre. The Equality Act 2010 requires reasonable adjustments for disability (hearing impairment), and the EYFS requires practitioners to provide an inclusive, communication-rich environment. EAL is not itself a special educational need, so assessment must distinguish language-learning from a possible additional language disorder, involving SLT and a teacher of the deaf rather than assuming delay.

A social-model response removes barriers: a quieter construction area, adults facing Yusuf when speaking, visual timetables, and a consistent key person. AAC (core board, Makaton) and hearing-aid checks empower him to choose and comment. For EAL, the silent period is respected; staff model language through commenting on his building ('You made a tall tower') rather than firing questions. Dual-language story times and a home-language greeting protect identity (Cummins; UNCRC Article 30).

Partnership is essential. Parents are experts on Yusuf's hearing equipment, home language and interests. The SENCO coordinates with audiology and, if needed, an EHC needs assessment, while the class teacher remains accountable for quality-first teaching. Integration (Yusuf merely present on the carpet) is not enough; inclusion means he can participate in the construction play he enjoys, with equity of access rather than identical provision.

Evaluation: no single strategy suffices. Pull-out English intervention that removes him from play could harm both language (which grows in meaningful contexts) and belonging. Over-identifying SEND because his English is new could lower expectations. The strongest approach combines reasonable adjustments, high-quality play-based interaction, home-language maintenance, and timely specialist assessment — reviewed in a short cycle so that what is not working is changed. QWC is shown through a structured argument, accurate terminology (reasonable adjustment, graduated approach, silent period, social model) and a clear judgement.`,markScheme:[`QWC: structure, specialist vocabulary, accurate terminology, coherent judgement.`,`Band 1 (1–5): Generic inclusion ideas; EAL and SEND conflated; little or no law/theory.`,`Band 2 (6–10): Some relevant strategies applied to Yusuf; at least one statute or theory; limited evaluation.`,`Band 3 (11–15): Distinguishes SEND and EAL; applies law, social model and language theory; evaluates alternative strategies; child's voice/family central.`,`Indicative strategies: key person, visuals, noise reduction, face-on talk, AAC, commenting, dual-language resources, construction as a context for talk.`,`Law: CFA 2014, SEND CoP 2015, Equality Act 2010, EYFS, UNCRC.`,`Theory: social vs medical model; Cummins; five stages / silent period; inclusion vs integration vs equity.`,`Multi-agency: SENCO, teacher of the deaf, SLT, audiology, parents.`,`Evaluation: warns against English-only, withdrawal that isolates, or mis-labelling EAL as SEND.`]}]}]},{id:`os-assignment-3`,title:`Occupational specialism — Assignment 3 Part 2 practice`,series:`Original EYE scenario practice`,paper:`OS`,duration:`2 hours (practice window)`,totalMarks:42,blurb:`Early Years Educator Assignment 3 Part 2 style. Four synoptic questions set in one nursery. Apply PO1–PO5: play and development, relationships, planning, safeguarding, and partnership.`,sections:[{name:`Willowbank Day Nursery — scenario questions`,elements:`PO1–PO5`,questions:[{id:`os-q1`,number:`1`,marks:6,ao:`AO2`,topicIds:[`5-4`,`5-3`,`2-5`],stem:`Outline a play-based activity to promote Leo's language and social development. Justify why the activity is age-appropriate.`,scenario:`Willowbank Day Nursery is a 52-place setting in a small English town with a baby room (0–2), toddler room (2–3) and pre-school (3–4). You are a Level 3 practitioner in the toddler room.

Leo is 2 years 4 months. He uses a handful of single words, often points or takes adults by the hand, and has begun to bite when another child takes a toy. He enjoys water play and wheeled toys. His key person suspects a speech and language delay and has, with parental consent, asked you to plan provision that will help while they await an SLT appointment.`,modelAnswer:`Plan a small-group water-play session for three toddlers, including Leo, with jugs, boats and animal figures. The adult kneels at the trough, comments on Leo's actions ('Leo's boat is sinking. Splash!') and models turn-taking with a visual 'my turn / your turn' card, rather than asking a string of test questions. A duplicate favourite toy reduces the need to bite to keep resources.

This is age-appropriate because 2-year-olds are typically in Piaget's sensorimotor-to-pre-operational shift and learn through sensory play; water is a schema-rich context (filling, transporting) that matches Leo's interest, so he is motivated to stay and hear language. Socially, toddlers are often in parallel play (Parten); a tiny group with an adult as more knowledgeable other (Vygotsky) scaffolds the first steps towards associative play without overwhelming him. Biting is treated as communication of limited language plus impulsivity, so the plan teaches an alternative ('stop' sign / 'mine') rather than only using sanctions.`,markScheme:[`Award marks for an outlined activity plus justification, up to 6.`,`Play-based, sensory, and matched to a stated interest (water / wheeled toys).`,`Language strategy: commenting, expanding, visuals, or dual resources — not interrogation.`,`Social strategy: tiny group, modelled turn-taking, adult scaffolding.`,`Age-appropriate justification with development theory (Piaget, Parten, Vygotsky, schemas).`,`Links biting to communication need and offers an alternative.`,`Practical and safe (ratios, water safety, hygiene) if mentioned.`]},{id:`os-q2`,number:`2`,marks:9,ao:`AO3`,topicIds:[`4-1`,`4-9`,`4-9`,`4-3`,`2-4`],stem:`Analyse Noah's likely needs in the pre-school room and explain how you would adapt the environment and daily routines using the social model of disability.`,scenario:`Noah is 4 years 1 month and has an EHC plan for autism. He uses a visual timetable, becomes distressed at unexpected indoor-to-outdoor transitions, and covers his ears when the room is noisy. He has a strong interest in lining up cars and will play alongside peers if they do not rearrange his lines. Two members of staff say 'he would cope better if he just joined carpet time like everyone else'.`,modelAnswer:`Noah's EHC plan means the setting has statutory duties under the Children and Families Act 2014 and the SEND Code of Practice 2015 to make the provision specified and to work to outcomes with Noah and his parents. Likely needs include differences in social communication, sensory processing (auditory hypersensitivity) and a need for predictability. These are not 'non-compliance'.

The social model locates disability in barriers: a sudden bell, a crowded carpet, and an insistence on identical provision. Adaptations: keep the visual timetable at Noah's height and preview outdoor time with a sand timer; offer a quieter construction/car area with noise-reducing options; allow him to join the carpet from the edge or for the final rhyme rather than forcing ten minutes in the middle; use a now-and-next board; and brief all staff so the 'join in like everyone else' view is challenged as integration without inclusion.

Equity, not identical treatment, is the aim: Noah accesses the same curriculum (UW, PSED, language) through cars and a structured outdoor job, which values his interest as a vehicle for learning. Staff should still gently stretch skills (a peer 'traffic officer' game) inside his ZPD, reviewing through assess–plan–do–review rather than lowering ambition.`,markScheme:[`Award marks for analysis of need plus social-model adaptations, up to 9.`,`Recognises EHC / CFA 2014 / SEND CoP duties.`,`Identifies communication, sensory and predictability needs without using deficit slang.`,`Explains the social model vs the 'he should just join in' medical/within-child view.`,`Environmental adaptations: visuals, noise, space, transition warnings.`,`Routine adaptations: flexible carpet time, now-and-next, planned interests.`,`Staff consistency and challenging attitudinal barriers.`,`Inclusion/equity distinct from mere integration; still offers stretch.`,`Involves parents / uses the EHC outcomes / graduated review.`]},{id:`os-q3`,number:`3`,marks:12,ao:`AO3`,topicIds:[`3-1`,`3-4`,`3-5`,`3-10`,`6-5`,`5-9`],stem:`Discuss the safeguarding actions you should take following the information about Freya. Analyse why each action matters.`,scenario:`Freya is 18 months. Her parents are separating. This morning a colleague tells you in the kitchen that they saw Freya's father shouting in the car park while holding Freya, and that Freya 'went stiff and hid her face'. Freya has been unsettled at handover for a week and has two small, unexplained bruises on her shins. Your colleague says they 'don't want to make a fuss because dad is always lovely at pick-up'. You are not the DSL.`,modelAnswer:`Treat the account as a safeguarding concern, not staffroom gossip. Record what was reported (who, when, where, exact words) and what you have observed about handover and the bruises, keeping fact separate from opinion. Bruises on shins in a mobile 18-month-old can be accidental, so do not jump to a conclusion — but unexplained marks plus an alleged incident of shouting while she was being held, plus a change in presentation, are a pattern that must be passed to the DSL the same day, in line with the setting's child protection policy, EYFS safeguarding requirements and Working Together.

Do not investigate by questioning Freya (she is 18 months), photographing injuries, or contacting father 'to hear his side' before the DSL decides. That could increase risk and is not the practitioner's role. If Freya were at immediate risk you would not delay: stay with her, call the DSL, and if needed children's social care or police. Share only with people who need to know; challenge the 'don't make a fuss' comment because professional curiosity and the duty to put the child's welfare first override adult embarrassment (KCSIE culture of vigilance, applied here via EYFS/inspectable practice).

The DSL will consider whether this is Early Help / child in need (s.17) or whether there is reasonable cause to suspect significant harm (s.47). You continue to offer Freya a calm key-person welcome, document ongoing presentation, and support the family without interrogating them. Analysis: inaction because an adult 'seems lovely' is a classic bias; domestic abuse and harm are not always visible at pick-up. Multi-agency decisions sit with the DSL and statutory services; your job is accurate, timely reporting and continued safe care.`,markScheme:[`Award marks for a discussed course of action with reasons, up to 12.`,`Records factual information; distinguishes observation from hearsay/opinion.`,`Reports to the DSL the same day; follows the setting's CP policy.`,`Does not investigate, question the toddler, photograph, or alert the parent first.`,`Considers immediate safety and when to stay with the child / call emergency services.`,`Confidentiality / need-to-know; challenges dismissal of the concern.`,`Bruises analysed cautiously (mobile toddler vs pattern with other indicators).`,`Links to Working Together, EYFS safeguarding, Children Act s.17/s.47.`,`Ongoing key-person support and further logging of presentation.`,`Recognises possible domestic abuse / impact of parental conflict on an infant.`,`Clear role boundary: practitioner reports; DSL/social care decide the threshold.`]},{id:`os-q4`,number:`4`,marks:15,ao:`AO3`,qwc:!0,topicIds:[`2-9`,`2-12`,`6-1`,`6-3`,`6-5`,`5-2`,`5-9`],stem:`Evaluate how Willowbank should work in partnership with Amira's family and with other professionals to support her settling in, language development and wellbeing. Make a reasoned judgement about priorities for the next six weeks.`,scenario:`Amira is 3 years 8 months and started in pre-school six weeks ago. Arabic is the home language. She is in a silent period in English, stays close to the door at arrival, and plays in parallel with a small world set. Her mother is keen to help but works early shifts and has said she worries that using Arabic at home will 'slow Amira's English'. A bilingual support assistant can visit for two sessions a week. Quality of written communication will be assessed.`,modelAnswer:`Level 3 / high-band response:

Priorities for six weeks should be relationship and wellbeing first, then language, because a child who is anxious will not take conversational risks. Bowlby's secure base and the EYFS key-person role suggest a consistent adult, a visual goodbye routine, and a shorter start to the day if needed. Settling is an expected transition that has not yet completed; treating Amira's door-hovering as misbehaviour would be a misread of development.

Partnership with family is the highest-leverage action. Staff should thank Amira's mother for her concern and explain, using an interpreter if needed, that maintaining Arabic supports attachment, identity and later English (Cummins' common underlying proficiency). Dual-language books, a recorded lullaby, and key Arabic words for comfort on a card for all staff reduce the silent period's isolation. Shift-work is a barrier (Element 5): offer a brief handover via a communication app with translated templates, and a fortnightly 10-minute phone call rather than only daytime workshops she cannot attend.

Other professionals: the bilingual assistant should work in play alongside Amira, not withdraw her from the room, modelling English and affirming Arabic. If concerns about more than additional-language acquisition remain after high-quality provision, the SENCO can observe with parental consent — but EAL must not be mis-labelled as SEND. SLT is not automatically required at six weeks of English exposure.

Evaluation: an English-only rule would be the weakest response. A six-week plan that floods Amira with flashcards but ignores the distressed arrival will also fail. The reasoned priority order is (1) key person and predictable goodbyes, (2) family partnership that protects home language, (3) visually supported, play-based English in context, (4) review with mother and bilingual assistant at six weeks using observation notes, not a formal language test. QWC is shown by a structured evaluation, accurate use of silent period, key person, Cummins, and a clear judgement.`,markScheme:[`QWC: coherent evaluation, specialist vocabulary, organised priorities.`,`Band 1 (1–5): Generic 'work with parents' and 'use visuals'; little theory; no judgement.`,`Band 2 (6–10): Relevant strategies for settling and EAL; some partnership ideas; limited evaluation of alternatives.`,`Band 3 (11–15): Secure theory (attachment, silent period, Cummins); barriers to partnership addressed; professionals used appropriately; clear six-week priorities and judgement.`,`Wellbeing/settling: key person, goodbye ritual, parallel play accepted, transition support.`,`Home language maintained; challenges the 'Arabic will slow English' myth.`,`Practical partnership given shift work: flexible contact, translated information.`,`Bilingual assistant in context; caution about mis-identifying EAL as SEND.`,`Evaluates weaker options (English-only, excessive withdrawal, ignoring distress).`]}]}]},{id:`esp-short`,title:`Employer-set project — short practice`,series:`Original ESP-style tasks`,paper:`ESP`,duration:`untimed practice (full ESP is 15 hours 45 minutes)`,totalMarks:45,blurb:`Three extended planning and evaluation tasks drawing on Element 1 Wider education sector and Element 7 Professional practice, plus the core skills. Use these to rehearse the 15-hour 45-minute ESP without the full controlled conditions.`,sections:[{name:`Riverside Federation brief`,elements:`1, 7 and professional practice`,questions:[{id:`esp-q1`,number:`1`,marks:15,ao:`AO3`,qwc:!0,topicIds:[`1-1`,`1-2`,`2-3`,`2-5`,`2-4`],stem:`Plan a four-week programme of educational provision for the nursery-to-Reception transition group. Justify your choices of setting, staffing roles and pedagogy with reference to the wider 0–19 context and the EYFS.`,scenario:`Riverside Primary and Nursery Federation (a maintained primary with an on-site nursery class) has asked T Level students to design a summer transition and early language project for children moving from nursery into Reception. The cohort of 26 includes children with EAL, two children with SEND support, and several who have not used the school site beyond the nursery wing. The employer wants the plan to show how nursery provision sits in the wider education landscape and how different roles will contribute.

Task 1 focuses on Element 1 (wider education sector): types of provision, who would lead the work, and why those pedagogical choices are defensible. Core skills CS1–CS4 will be evidenced in later tasks.`,modelAnswer:`Level 3 / high-band response:

The programme sits at the hinge between the EYFS (statutory from birth to the end of Reception) and the National Curriculum, which children will meet in Year 1. That matters: the four weeks should protect play-based, prime-area learning rather than importing formal Key Stage 1 pedagogy too early — a common sector error. Provision is therefore planned as a 'bridge class' using the nursery and Reception environments on alternate days in week 1–2, then mostly Reception in weeks 3–4, so children map the new microsystem (Bronfenbrenner) gradually.

Roles: the Reception teacher holds overall curriculum responsibility; the early years educator/key person from nursery attends the first fortnight to preserve attachment; a teaching assistant supports SEND adjustments; the SENCO advises on the two children with support plans; lunchtime staff are briefed because unstructured times are high-risk for behaviour and wellbeing. This demonstrates understanding of career roles and why Level 3 educators are not interchangeable with unqualified helpers.

Pedagogy: a play-based, language-rich approach drawing on Vygotsky (adults as more knowledgeable others in the construction and role-play areas), Bruner's scaffolding that fades across the four weeks, and Froebelian/outdoor learning in the shared garden so physical development and self-regulation are not squeezed out. Daily core books and rhymes provide a spiral curriculum. Systematic phonics is introduced in short, multi-sensory bursts matching Reception practice, but not as hour-long desk sessions, because that would ignore developmental patterns in the final year of EYFS.

Justification against alternatives: a 'taster day' only is too thin for children who have never crossed the playground; a sudden full-time Reception week in June may overwhelm those with SEND or EAL. Linking to 0–19, the plan also notes how successful early transitions reduce later disengagement — a sector concern through KS3–4. QWC: structured plan, named frameworks, clear justification.`,markScheme:[`QWC assessed: organisation, specialist vocabulary, justified argument.`,`Band 1 (1–5): A list of activities with little reference to EYFS, roles or 0–19 context.`,`Band 2 (6–10): A recognisable four-week shape; some pedagogy and roles; partial justification.`,`Band 3 (11–15): Coherent programme; EYFS vs National Curriculum understood; roles differentiated; pedagogy justified with theory; alternatives evaluated.`,`Wider context: maintained nursery/Reception, other provision types acknowledged if relevant, progression 0–19.`,`Roles: teacher, EYE/key person, TA, SENCO, lunchtime staff — not generic 'staff'.`,`Pedagogy: play, scaffolding, language, outdoor, carefully pitched phonics.`,`Justification: why this, why not a one-off taster or an overly formal model.`]},{id:`esp-q2`,number:`2`,marks:15,ao:`AO3`,qwc:!0,topicIds:[`7-1`,`7-2`,`7-3`,`7-4`,`2-12`,`4-9`],stem:`Using child development theory, analyse how your planned provision will support language, attachment and a successful expected transition for this cohort, including children with EAL and SEND. Then explain the diagnostic, formative and summative evidence you would collect.`,scenario:`Continue from the Riverside Federation brief. Task 2 draws on Element 7 Professional practice as well as the child-development knowledge you need to justify the plan: show that it is grounded in expected patterns of development, attachment and language, and that you can observe, assess and report progress. Refer to named children types in the cohort (EAL, SEND, children new to the site) rather than writing only in generalities.`,modelAnswer:`Level 3 / high-band response:

The move from nursery to Reception is an expected transition, but it still disrupts secure bases. Bowlby and the key-person principle imply that taking a familiar adult into Reception for the first fortnight reduces protest and freeze responses. For 4-year-olds (pre-operational; still developing self-regulation), a visual timetable and a photograph book of the new toilets, garden and teacher support the child's ability to hold the new setting in mind — object permanence is long established, but emotional object constancy under stress is not.

Language: children at this age typically use sentences and can retell, but EAL learners may be in early acquisition or a silent period. Provision that relies on carpet talk alone will exclude them. Construction, snack commentary and core-book props allow receptive language to lead, matching the usual receptive-ahead-of-expressive pattern. Cummins' BICS will grow in playground play; CALP is not the four-week target. For SEND, reasonable adjustments (a quieter entry, AAC or now-and-next) apply the social model so the transition does not become a barrier that creates additional need.

Friendships (Parten; Selman) are a protective factor. Planning paired 'buddies' from the existing nursery group, and teaching the new class a joining-in song, supports PSED so children are not solely dependent on adults. Analysis of risk: children who have not used the wider site may show behaviour that looks like non-compliance but is disorientation; staff who analyse development will co-regulate rather than escalate. Unexpected additional transitions (a key person absent, a late EHCP review) should have a contingency: a second named adult. The plan succeeds if, by week 4, children can use the Reception environment with their new teacher as a secure base and have a bank of shared language routines — not if they can sit in silence for 20 minutes.`,markScheme:[`QWC: analytical structure, accurate developmental vocabulary, linked to the plan.`,`Band 1 (1–5): Descriptive child development with weak links to the programme.`,`Band 2 (6–10): Some theory applied to language or transition; EAL/SEND mentioned briefly.`,`Band 3 (11–15): Attachment, language and transition analysed together; EAL and SEND integrated; age/stage accurate; risks and protective factors weighed.`,`Attachment / key person / secure base.`,`Expected vs additional stressors in this transition.`,`Language: receptive/expressive, silent period, play as context, Cummins if used well.`,`SEND: social model / adjustments, not 'they must cope'.`,`Friendships and self-regulation as part of successful transition.`,`Clear success criteria grounded in development, not just compliance.`]},{id:`esp-q3`,number:`3`,marks:15,ao:`AO3`,qwc:!0,topicIds:[`6-1`,`6-3`,`6-5`,`7-5`,`7-6`,`7-7`,`7-3`],stem:`Evaluate how you would work with parents, colleagues and other professionals to deliver the project, and how you would reflect on and improve your own practice. Justify the evidence you would collect.`,scenario:`Task 3 covers Element 6 Working in partnership and Element 7 Professional practice. The employer will ask how you would avoid tokenistic parent contact, keep professional boundaries, collect valid observation evidence, and show that you can improve the programme during the four weeks using a named model of reflection — not only write a neat plan in advance.`,modelAnswer:`Level 3 / high-band response:

Parents are partners, not an audience for a finished plan. A launch stay-and-play, translated summaries, and a choice of communication routes address barriers (shifts, EAL, previous negative school experience). Unique parental knowledge (comforters, home language, SEND strategies already used) should change the plan in week 1 — that is genuine partnership. Boundaries still apply: no personal social-media friendships, no discussing other children, consent before any photograph for the transition book (UK GDPR).

Colleagues: a short daily debrief between Reception teacher, nursery educator and TA keeps the team consistent. The SENCO and, where involved, SLT or bilingual assistant attend a midway review. Collaborative working fails if visiting professionals are not briefed on the play-based pedagogy and pull children out at random; a lead professional (class teacher) coordinates times.

Evidence: short narrative observations of participation, language samples, a simple Leuven-style wellbeing/involvement snapshot, and parent comments. This is formative assessment to reshape week 3–4, not a summative test. Avoid excessive tracking that takes adults off the floor.

Reflection: use Gibbs after week 1 (description, feelings about a distressed child, evaluation, analysis, action). Schön's reflection-in-action covers mid-session changes (shortening a tour). A mentor observation and a personal development target (for example giving more processing time to EAL learners) show Element 7. Evaluation: a beautiful display with no change to adult–child interaction would be failure. Success is that partnership and reflection produce visible adaptations — a quieter arrival, a father who works nights getting a voice-note update, a phonics burst moved later because observations showed children were still in a settling dip. That is professional practice aligned to the ESP's synoptic intent.`,markScheme:[`QWC: evaluative tone, structure covering partnership, boundaries, evidence, reflection.`,`Band 1 (1–5): Generic 'talk to parents and reflect'; little on barriers, evidence or models.`,`Band 2 (6–10): Some partnership methods and a reflective model; evidence listed but weakly justified.`,`Band 3 (11–15): Barriers and boundaries handled; multi-agency coordination; assessment evidence justified; reflective model applied; clear judgement of what would count as improvement.`,`Parents: two-way communication, flexibility, home languages, SEND knowledge.`,`Professional boundaries and GDPR/consent.`,`Colleagues and other agencies with a lead coordinator.`,`Formative observation/wellbeing evidence, not battery testing.`,`Named reflective model plus mentor/CPD.`,`Evaluation of tokenistic alternatives.`]}]}]}];export{n,t as r,e as t};